'symbols' - load CPU symbols & their addresses
Usage:  symbols <code|data|name> -- list symbols
	symbols <prg|free> -- load/free symbols
	        <filename> [<T offset> [<D offset> <B offset>]]
	symbols <autoload|match> -- toggle symbol options

	'name' command lists the currently loaded symbols, sorted by name.
	'code' and 'data' commands list them sorted by address; 'code' lists
	only TEXT symbols, 'data' lists DATA/BSS/ABS symbols.

	By default, symbols are loaded from the currently executing program's
	binary when entering the debugger, IF program is started through
	GEMDOS HD, and they're freed when that program terminates.

	That corresponds to 'prg' command which loads (DRI/GST or a.out
	format) symbol table from the last program executed through
	the GEMDOS HD emulation.

	'free' command removes the loaded symbols.

	If program lacks symbols, or it's not run through the GEMDOS HD
	emulation, user can ask symbols to be loaded from a file that's
	an unstripped version of the binary. Or from an ASCII symbols file
	produced by the 'nm' and (Hatari) 'gst2ascii' tools.

	With ASCII symbols files, given non-zero offset(s) are added to
	the text (T), data (D) and BSS (B) symbols.  Typically one uses
	TEXT variable, sometimes also DATA & BSS, variables for this.

	'autoload [on|off]' command toggle/set whether debugger will load
	symbols for currently executing (GEMDOS HD) program automatically
	on entering the debugger (i.e. replace earlier loaded symbols),
	and free them when program terminates.  It needs to be disabled
	to debug memory-resident programs used by other programs.

	'match' command toggles whether TAB completion matches all symbols,
	or only symbol types that should be relevant for given command.
